// Generated by pnpm narrate. Keep recordings and captions in sync.
const NARRATION = {
  "version": 1,
  "chapters": [
    [
      {
        "text": "Welcome! Every game you have ever played began with a small idea and a few instructions. Today, your idea starts here.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-01-part-1.76f4e5e15f42.mp3",
        "duration": 7.944,
        "bytes": 63552,
        "sha256": "76f4e5e15f42c8077a622c8cf832b492745a01d9a85503b5db47ef162f97c56d",
        "sourceHash": "4b89837c99f5697d7036d6c9f9f093b05cd79f275ffd64f57284465673bf67de"
      },
      {
        "text": "This rectangle is your canvas. Think of it as a tiny digital art studio. You decide what appears inside it.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-01-part-2.414be9df2168.mp3",
        "duration": 7.32,
        "bytes": 58560,
        "sha256": "414be9df216889cd6e71bc03b2e9c9563be4457e6a9008b0af87bc4a6b049ce0",
        "sourceHash": "a24ce9d2869733d9a120d08d0d4e63614750a9eb95d29099fd79ac332106a4d4"
      },
      {
        "text": "The setup function gets your studio ready. createCanvas makes a space four hundred eighty pixels wide, and three hundred twenty pixels tall.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-01-part-3.a1278c5581d8.mp3",
        "duration": 9.12,
        "bytes": 72960,
        "sha256": "a1278c5581d8da75a6f4be09f25fc01dfdcd7a14a9517458300457a8915ec3a9",
        "sourceHash": "e697623a0ed31776dca84dd913919c0733a6cb2fbee90739289423c84d85657b"
      },
      {
        "text": "The draw function paints the picture. background chooses the paper color. fill chooses the paint. circle draws your first shape.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-01-part-4.f7110c0b78ba.mp3",
        "duration": 8.064,
        "bytes": 64512,
        "sha256": "f7110c0b78baae59813a7b5e30aae85b776826979ec4ddf025dee0fc6482990f",
        "sourceHash": "9616277e0b9b12c853366dc6a4b4c424201eed02d7c46752c3e93d9c6ac74840"
      },
      {
        "text": "The three numbers in circle mean horizontal position, vertical position, and size. Change the last number and watch the circle grow.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-01-part-5.cecbe1271c91.mp3",
        "duration": 9.024,
        "bytes": 72192,
        "sha256": "cecbe1271c91d580ddd16d6f1863be595f156988b2e5846d89fbb2c827a175dc",
        "sourceHash": "11f4288a667988d93808bf3682280bf65a545068cce58056e85e6cc540b521e5"
      },
      {
        "text": "You do not need to memorize everything. Change one number, run the code, and notice what happens. That is how programmers discover things.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-01-part-6.4a45a2632bca.mp3",
        "duration": 8.88,
        "bytes": 71040,
        "sha256": "4a45a2632bca164279df79b335c08dbf9d27a139bbd17850b1dfa2f3c842ab79",
        "sourceHash": "2ba4835818e5db5e9a0b8bee8d646e6ee8fe75492263d3fec1ecb1f04790d6c0"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Imagine sending a letter to one tiny spot on your canvas. It needs an address, and that address is made of two numbers.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-02-part-1.88900f6682eb.mp3",
        "duration": 7.968,
        "bytes": 63744,
        "sha256": "88900f6682eb5d48d6e76da0aa4628b7f00a6c3b57780c4a92be118d752ffb66",
        "sourceHash": "6196806df31934a670569dac5cc22bfd61bb6857268da17ec45b14db59590a9a"
      },
      {
        "text": "The first number is x. It measures how far right you go, starting at the left edge of the canvas.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-02-part-2.812263f514c2.mp3",
        "duration": 6.312,
        "bytes": 50496,
        "sha256": "812263f514c24fbb93308fd14675c17114188498e48ab29309883e483e669338",
        "sourceHash": "74d20911a1e7321c10a897e65c0ae7fcbcfa72e8ce3a6944c7e1e0e45a23f15b"
      },
      {
        "text": "The second number is y. It measures how far down you go, starting at the top. That might feel different from math class.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-02-part-3.578247dff12a.mp3",
        "duration": 7.512,
        "bytes": 60096,
        "sha256": "578247dff12acd8a61ec549b6062da6911da1bcd23d3d694642c5f1e59116dc0",
        "sourceHash": "ba52fc4c76517f3160872bccf5771b4e86e88eb1d4d34f34280c272b61ccb10d"
      },
      {
        "text": "The top left corner has the address zero, zero. The middle of our canvas is two hundred forty, one hundred sixty.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-02-part-4.9bd9727f77e6.mp3",
        "duration": 7.656,
        "bytes": 61248,
        "sha256": "9bd9727f77e60d6149103fac438aa45466f74e21cbbd9ccd433287a7d8c5f7f3",
        "sourceHash": "4ab251c5de73499f09054bc0b1d026ae49892d26f558eab0280d13ee875ce4b3"
      },
      {
        "text": "A circle uses its center as its address. A rectangle usually uses its top left corner, followed by width and height.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-02-part-5.8d9459358fa5.mp3",
        "duration": 8.088,
        "bytes": 64704,
        "sha256": "8d9459358fa5372263b9d036263ef40bddcb783656281656763364e03b1b9de7",
        "sourceHash": "134b40ea0424c97632be91b72da93ad6e0b61dcd657cbe4d0d35e5bd65ea14ef"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try moving a shape before you run the code. Make a prediction, test it, and compare. You are already thinking like a programmer.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-02-part-6.9ee684b8dfbd.mp3",
        "duration": 8.016,
        "bytes": 64128,
        "sha256": "9ee684b8dfbda79fc61583cdd47af780db07993b0d208cb7b3e0455c6ca5c069",
        "sourceHash": "3a35d71b1499d9a9dd8616de87d282bea8c44d9dfce5b127e51a74ad381a3205"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A game needs a personality, and color is a wonderful place to start. Your code has its own paint palette.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-03-part-1.6821064c95f4.mp3",
        "duration": 7.128,
        "bytes": 57024,
        "sha256": "6821064c95f4cd0f99b54724442b69775f61c8e9a0efd73a896d9480d9a4ce1b",
        "sourceHash": "17e6c91a2aa049106d142ada2f67be474d787a6330b8ce81077a37ab6fa779d0"
      },
      {
        "text": "background paints over the entire canvas. That is why we usually put it first, before drawing any shapes.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-03-part-2.afab0c9f52e9.mp3",
        "duration": 7.056,
        "bytes": 56448,
        "sha256": "afab0c9f52e982f049e25ec258631063fbba8ee8854f424435b3a5090cf12e11",
        "sourceHash": "174a2b874d1547d557a3e40374b2f4d7a7023f83361ba4c5e86cf174d561069a"
      },
      {
        "text": "fill picks the paint for the shapes that follow. It keeps using that color until you choose another one.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-03-part-3.9da25f356c0c.mp3",
        "duration": 6.312,
        "bytes": 50496,
        "sha256": "9da25f356c0cfd20ee4554d0a3ce5e66c4636ab552f721a351782ae41de3b19b",
        "sourceHash": "7a2c78aee4e60f6e23681294ba53678e5dd72fb031f56068f84e252e09fabba2"
      },
      {
        "text": "A color can be a name, like coral, or a six digit code. You can also mix red, green, and blue with numbers.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-03-part-4.360d73742396.mp3",
        "duration": 7.296,
        "bytes": 58368,
        "sha256": "360d73742396ef296a375fc0967202ce4ea13c273005e192c12cc55eb910c073",
        "sourceHash": "32ec6a5ced8a78ee53e82da8f926388e8b6358504eb708885cd42088143bdd7c"
      },
      {
        "text": "The drawing order matters. The computer paints from top to bottom, so a later shape can cover an earlier shape.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-03-part-5.73918781aebd.mp3",
        "duration": 6.984,
        "bytes": 55872,
        "sha256": "73918781aebd59d27578435fe3d7bbec03c7ce6d58b14d046c164c18f9b26bbf",
        "sourceHash": "2adfcffebb2adbe63eee72b963adff6036a6b6f108bfe4979df694c8ca1894f2"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try making a sunset, a planet, or a team color palette. There is no single right look. You get to choose.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-03-part-6.e94e3ae3e7f7.mp3",
        "duration": 6.912,
        "bytes": 55296,
        "sha256": "e94e3ae3e7f7f41121d08769d8714f7e08a719da69d1a18959d9fc45045f8859",
        "sourceHash": "d032a46e920b7556cd0ef9b4ee2e88811bf6178c2705930d8127f09c89587609"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "How does a still picture become a game? The secret is to draw lots of pictures, very quickly, with tiny changes between them.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-04-part-1.72fac518ba69.mp3",
        "duration": 8.232,
        "bytes": 65856,
        "sha256": "72fac518ba69e16f07565feccabfa31714a2551f742fd899d5b894b0f1e42096",
        "sourceHash": "50a773ecd95499d4d9e19a3d7650b31015ca0915f6daf17df1b127f5b5792eb5"
      },
      {
        "text": "setup runs once at the beginning. It prepares the canvas, chooses starting settings, and gets everything ready.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-04-part-2.ddb748abf484.mp3",
        "duration": 7.2,
        "bytes": 57600,
        "sha256": "ddb748abf4849de6fdcf30b9f158c381155efa98ff8e1f55bbaaf5e8ca7bb984",
        "sourceHash": "7b9761eda213671a43ee2bcd62d22ddcb9e298239c5942342130119e97a85a6f"
      },
      {
        "text": "draw runs over and over. Each time it runs, it paints one frame. Many frames together create the feeling of movement.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-04-part-3.a2db1746dc3a.mp3",
        "duration": 8.328,
        "bytes": 66624,
        "sha256": "a2db1746dc3aa7ebf29270579014db63d4bf177e1e676645cbbf03e9e3c8d64b",
        "sourceHash": "0dcc7e8e0da7befed141f4fff718e893f399a34ac30818b3f27b6b0cee771543"
      },
      {
        "text": "frameCount is a number p5 gives us. It increases each time draw runs, so we can use it to move our circle.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-04-part-4.3617a61f7789.mp3",
        "duration": 7.656,
        "bytes": 61248,
        "sha256": "3617a61f7789ab1186b7883cd47dcca3b1032d7a9f8167e5288a2f70ed9b8e67",
        "sourceHash": "0c8b761a0f2bd261f56b59fcb79ac2b64ac341db531ecbdf54c728c996528eaa"
      },
      {
        "text": "background clears away the last frame. Without it, the old circles stay behind and make a trail, like a long exposure photo.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-04-part-5.102c711f188f.mp3",
        "duration": 8.448,
        "bytes": 67584,
        "sha256": "102c711f188f67f0fc81243bd6c5ced655112b82b8c5519a9024c034bf27a6ef",
        "sourceHash": "8241c8c84d4933fcb1228358341f032add35d75039ecbdc78851e1a9e1180852"
      },
      {
        "text": "Move an instruction between setup and draw, then restart. Ask yourself whether it should happen once, or keep happening.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-04-part-6.de4b68e86c3b.mp3",
        "duration": 7.704,
        "bytes": 61632,
        "sha256": "de4b68e86c3bf3ee201a6c69a1713bd742e0d90e0e75f8189803c6a0f70a35bb",
        "sourceHash": "1d1bae157b352a4e23a728ffc701d920f91eb7f371ce3f195f0785dfcb987051"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Imagine a small labeled box. You can put a number inside, look at it later, and replace it whenever you need. That is a variable.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-05-part-1.f7612825f770.mp3",
        "duration": 8.736,
        "bytes": 69888,
        "sha256": "f7612825f770e544b43ce7422dcbfbfac9b78401203633856cf14a06ce6dde78",
        "sourceHash": "894ea9d38605d17c30a61093abadaf60fb4683ba38f7ae14ad17e9cc72ddad46"
      },
      {
        "text": "The word let creates a variable. After it comes a name, an equals sign, and the value you want to store.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-05-part-2.2fa020b1aeb3.mp3",
        "duration": 6.888,
        "bytes": 55104,
        "sha256": "2fa020b1aeb3813fa879f7e85f7d2e53b02fdd9233100e0c474652537323609a",
        "sourceHash": "425901b73747f3ab12644537e4e7b13cac3ad9170ba32b98c0b065e53f7b6504"
      },
      {
        "text": "Our ballX variable remembers where the ball belongs. ballSize remembers how big it should be. Clear names help us understand the code.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-05-part-3.ee623e4feee4.mp3",
        "duration": 9.168,
        "bytes": 73344,
        "sha256": "ee623e4feee40086e30f235c2b25aa3104bc58ac8e6a63c7fa805a81da7e2a6d",
        "sourceHash": "cafd77babca1150280e2261d944f830b72657f79b345b25785e394a09558c8f8"
      },
      {
        "text": "When the computer sees ballSize inside circle, it looks up the number in that box and uses that number to draw.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-05-part-4.edab2d719e5e.mp3",
        "duration": 7.512,
        "bytes": 60096,
        "sha256": "edab2d719e5eaa4141d0e33f8a1052720737129c113b5624bd90bb3d675613d1",
        "sourceHash": "41302a9217e00ae128b1f074028397b5fe0fb56b33918034d1a6bd771c495102"
      },
      {
        "text": "A single variable can appear in many places. Change its starting value once, and all those places use the new value.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-05-part-5.81398d2ead61.mp3",
        "duration": 7.728,
        "bytes": 61824,
        "sha256": "81398d2ead61f1d6694b4e7af43d032235e52d4b4adb48a6c7f06967d7a99add",
        "sourceHash": "8f4b65a7f6483aaa732c68e877b615dfa43c28a0ebc74ab785d112636f9c1b20"
      },
      {
        "text": "Later, variables will remember the score and how many lives remain. For now, experiment with position and size. Your boxes are ready.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-05-part-6.dd2a1fe27f1c.mp3",
        "duration": 9.36,
        "bytes": 74880,
        "sha256": "dd2a1fe27f1c5c903c7aeff4dfc8855857263eb77da5c0ad6014d57170d55eb3",
        "sourceHash": "d0cca9d250625e441f2267e21c7f44e5c954f494a37a64f13e450181180aadde"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "You already know how to place a circle. To make it move, we need to change that position each time a new frame is drawn.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-06-part-1.028e432827ad.mp3",
        "duration": 7.728,
        "bytes": 61824,
        "sha256": "028e432827ad3ba840c8e7181e231099883191c8354548d1b10be50f41a85af3",
        "sourceHash": "0a9fd43a244dcdadac7b413e672fadddebddae0df89d950e8e24a68659e0deda"
      },
      {
        "text": "The variable x stores the current position. The variable speed stores how many pixels the ball should move during each frame.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-06-part-2.cfc6926fe0eb.mp3",
        "duration": 8.064,
        "bytes": 64512,
        "sha256": "cfc6926fe0ebceca291f463be827b0657c8c14d4391a455d2194fd373b230409",
        "sourceHash": "bad799a50e1a58793cda75ea669052137397ecb556d72f4aa2ec23cf3311d14e"
      },
      {
        "text": "The instruction x plus equals speed means take the old x, add speed, and store the result back in x.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-06-part-3.3dde5e9b39da.mp3",
        "duration": 7.344,
        "bytes": 58752,
        "sha256": "3dde5e9b39daf5d0b336a005efa81662481b9f9b17f97853b2644ed2aa7fdd18",
        "sourceHash": "1354e229943b177652757385b69fdd36994b373fdbe51345882a7a847cedacfc"
      },
      {
        "text": "Now draw repeats the same three steps. Clear the canvas, update the position, and paint the ball in its new spot.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-06-part-4.c8c33c652fbb.mp3",
        "duration": 7.488,
        "bytes": 59904,
        "sha256": "c8c33c652fbbf347b3e51226fae75843542c75ed0e9eea1671ee16c69b3bf180",
        "sourceHash": "9c42dce59f3a2755ab94946a994bf28c63b053fba7ddbd04b5ab63f92f8d9095"
      },
      {
        "text": "Bigger steps make faster movement. A negative speed moves left instead of right. The percent operator lets this example wrap around.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-06-part-5.48c230a96d27.mp3",
        "duration": 9.168,
        "bytes": 73344,
        "sha256": "48c230a96d27ea260966777000359f6c7462cd2ad5ceecceae70b41fff7be05f",
        "sourceHash": "f48e8c979008428df9f9549760edbc002f66c09e8f5a6a6fa1947818c1bcbf9a"
      },
      {
        "text": "This is a simple simulation: a little state changing over time. Soon this same moving circle will become the ball in your game.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-06-part-6.a8c4da39c52e.mp3",
        "duration": 8.4,
        "bytes": 67200,
        "sha256": "a8c4da39c52e9f11017f10e0c8163af17fbd8cdf7fefe25a1eca070014d17655",
        "sourceHash": "b3057e170a645411a2a9e43981f9996e303d07fd7fc347ae3fb90e1cfcab4fc9"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A game constantly asks questions. Did the ball hit a wall? Is the player out of lives? An if statement lets your code make decisions.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-07-part-1.7defaa0bbc78.mp3",
        "duration": 8.616,
        "bytes": 68928,
        "sha256": "7defaa0bbc785e3cb8c451ca12bfa8ee9f9c31891555368bec4768d7bd716015",
        "sourceHash": "c31135f4f3a0befeb6ba6858804e2983a98d2097ddc83308a856644dc7d76e85"
      },
      {
        "text": "A condition is a question that has a true or false answer. For example, is x greater than the middle of the canvas?",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-07-part-2.18deb9f849bb.mp3",
        "duration": 7.632,
        "bytes": 61056,
        "sha256": "18deb9f849bbf9bb3becd051a9e836cfe0cb8d2b2c095fdebb500c43c9266d5a",
        "sourceHash": "bc0068867f0e084d2a24c9054c8b5fd7739b2a077ae6f4392e03dc4a718fbd5a"
      },
      {
        "text": "When the answer is true, the instructions inside the first pair of curly brackets run. When it is false, they are skipped.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-07-part-3.1d61f6e4a582.mp3",
        "duration": 7.56,
        "bytes": 60480,
        "sha256": "1d61f6e4a582f511f81e8baa8dbfd9c6648ba406dc5ab094bfcb52be7f2fa6ee",
        "sourceHash": "64ecfa72f6c566fcf20de757bad1f4c138bd351ffc583671ccd454a672c2825f"
      },
      {
        "text": "An else block gives your code another path. In this sketch, the ball gets one color before the threshold, and another after it.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-07-part-4.85565267684a.mp3",
        "duration": 8.016,
        "bytes": 64128,
        "sha256": "85565267684a9889326bc3ddc16016791893e35c3bce8a58c5a35423ac311269",
        "sourceHash": "5e440e90ca242df685626b9bf4e85455db915bdab1b19c589fb38e94bf7f2aff"
      },
      {
        "text": "The greater than symbol compares two numbers. Two equals signs, or three equals signs for strict equality, ask whether values match.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-07-part-5.c54dded20018.mp3",
        "duration": 8.808,
        "bytes": 70464,
        "sha256": "c54dded20018ae55ed9aaa4b96cbbab554ed2b0a832d889c3914db7182733289",
        "sourceHash": "54e270e10c08035e35db851e47cd2f143dc04f687bc5e939b2e2a12c2b48640a"
      },
      {
        "text": "Move the threshold or choose different colors. Watch the moment your condition changes its answer. You have just created a rule.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-07-part-6.4d076d5ff45d.mp3",
        "duration": 8.136,
        "bytes": 65088,
        "sha256": "4d076d5ff45db1f02ef7f2cee3eaaeae350143574b21914e08b91387078d7c71",
        "sourceHash": "ac1f47807c612611c6d70b56d41b79578525b8f866df1118fc42dbab568a9e97"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Our ball can move, but a game ball should not disappear through the wall. Let us give the canvas some boundaries.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-08-part-1.f2cd7b089e69.mp3",
        "duration": 6.84,
        "bytes": 54720,
        "sha256": "f2cd7b089e695360aaeb6e6c266f918201cbaa474d88f606b627ccbcc5aae947",
        "sourceHash": "7a25c09d5ed914e1bfc96073b7e066c426cb3eba88c63ccd8e289614c077471e"
      },
      {
        "text": "Velocity tells us how fast something moves and which direction it is going. A positive horizontal velocity moves right; a negative one moves left.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-08-part-2.02b74a63b81f.mp3",
        "duration": 10.488,
        "bytes": 83904,
        "sha256": "02b74a63b81f0bbfc4197dd47cff58d6925201fc0a8400b37cdd11ccf25bc0e2",
        "sourceHash": "f95504569541354166e99c7eee16def20a94545ab5ccf87ce0b46e86b5db6b24"
      },
      {
        "text": "We check whether the center is closer to a wall than the ball radius. The radius is half the diameter of the circle.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-08-part-3.331ce38028ca.mp3",
        "duration": 7.608,
        "bytes": 60864,
        "sha256": "331ce38028ca28d079edbaec89d46261bca5c6541c8348623cd94fb247faba58",
        "sourceHash": "39acb866dad349d2c69d5176c283e8ed0fb362177da918a064bfe2be34251e4e"
      },
      {
        "text": "At an edge, multiplying speed by negative one reverses the direction. The next frame moves the ball back into the canvas.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-08-part-4.5405d821a317.mp3",
        "duration": 8.592,
        "bytes": 68736,
        "sha256": "5405d821a3172b7cd09df2c5138f645d5a69f2ff06de501f47dc4b6df37b55c7",
        "sourceHash": "5187a4a14409af4b0d3748cc9f538214a64263b0ee53ad771045a9999fda4b5b"
      },
      {
        "text": "We also clamp the position inside the boundary. This keeps the ball from getting stuck beyond the edge and flipping direction forever.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-08-part-5.888061f0ac90.mp3",
        "duration": 8.112,
        "bytes": 64896,
        "sha256": "888061f0ac90ccad212e03faf20f5081a77b43474c26e80d9ec1a787689b9a6e",
        "sourceHash": "cba8d5bcd3cb2ce86c6820f793aa87e1b02cfc4f2a8d13dfb329e115748f6963"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try a different starting speed or a much bigger ball. One rule handles all of them. This is a small, useful model of physics.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-08-part-6.707680aa9e56.mp3",
        "duration": 8.4,
        "bytes": 67200,
        "sha256": "707680aa9e5651b1c51290a142c9d63bc3556ee727771a472bc5e22409c9b066",
        "sourceHash": "57956385efc69c2d5fe0126ac0cef816128e58c073a63b9becbd2c509f74b2c1"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A picture becomes interactive when it responds to something you do. That something is called input. Let us start with your mouse.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-09-part-1.006c83c684ca.mp3",
        "duration": 7.968,
        "bytes": 63744,
        "sha256": "006c83c684ca12faa903d69ef00ab5d82e76c94aca9237ad407e5282c8fda923",
        "sourceHash": "aa47a2c5c1f66302a31b74f46550772e56743ba3a06a84b025f3793c1a3529e2"
      },
      {
        "text": "p5 updates two special variables for us: mouseX and mouseY. They store the pointer position relative to the canvas, not the whole page.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-09-part-2.e51638fca4a8.mp3",
        "duration": 9.816,
        "bytes": 78528,
        "sha256": "e51638fca4a82dd64a7a6091ed116fe0c44c4d358746a2509dc071145de1695c",
        "sourceHash": "47fb3a04fd36680a8146fdd4175b14846e8892eca10598523f4c6d325d02dc82"
      },
      {
        "text": "Use mouseX as the circle x coordinate, and mouseY as its y coordinate. Now the circle follows you each frame.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-09-part-3.5d03a5f49532.mp3",
        "duration": 8.4,
        "bytes": 67200,
        "sha256": "5d03a5f495323c64598368106c9b750a33420423b55d2d06015fe1add607ad6f",
        "sourceHash": "940564c7841f2405fdbfa487e96e510ac2fdba94dc798aec260862605b03d631"
      },
      {
        "text": "This little connection is the beginning of a game controller. Your hand moves, the values change, and the canvas responds.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-09-part-4.1c23f19a0d2b.mp3",
        "duration": 7.824,
        "bytes": 62592,
        "sha256": "1c23f19a0d2bd2389571e6ea4767c9ae3cf1feaf472643745c6421670d70e47e",
        "sourceHash": "4844b9a619d8b4743e233cfaa2db4aa0680acebd1b34bd12c31d66c5edd16aeb"
      },
      {
        "text": "You can follow only one direction, or add an offset to draw a companion shape nearby. Input is just another number you can use.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-09-part-5.73d6c11bcdf8.mp3",
        "duration": 9,
        "bytes": 72000,
        "sha256": "73d6c11bcdf80349eff0ec9067e6330032d4cf3bfed17b7201bde04a5d6abde3",
        "sourceHash": "e3dfd504222a83708acd9217387a79442c023e9293d42d48d1514757a1dbfa62"
      },
      {
        "text": "Move the pointer around your canvas after running the code. Try making a giant cursor, a tiny cursor, or two shapes that travel together.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-09-part-6.8709a203c74c.mp3",
        "duration": 9.192,
        "bytes": 73536,
        "sha256": "8709a203c74c2385858d2d314b51b8a983a07db91fbea4e2d243d89960245ab9",
        "sourceHash": "95ae70007f5dc8464558745c5c25035d7e29aad5853e9ab7d5bcb0e4eef2c593"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A keyboard is another way for a player to speak to a game. Today the left and right arrow keys will steer a paddle.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-10-part-1.a0603b200380.mp3",
        "duration": 7.56,
        "bytes": 60480,
        "sha256": "a0603b20038051efa792c3e7772a462dd231b74106fb0eb99ea200f5af91b30e",
        "sourceHash": "501c92fc3de53e74e45783c6ef8916b2d50cd08ce8696d285e7325460d799031"
      },
      {
        "text": "First, click inside the canvas so it receives your key presses. Then hold an arrow and watch the paddle move.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-10-part-2.24e279135649.mp3",
        "duration": 7.128,
        "bytes": 57024,
        "sha256": "24e27913564995eaede7251eea73a79c5385cfc4b15c7d471bff18e0e2f457f8",
        "sourceHash": "367400e6e8042e61a4d94c0690ed79c0ed8020b609eb48a04b8329e15c5a603e"
      },
      {
        "text": "keyIsDown asks whether a particular key is currently held. We ask that question inside draw, so the movement continues every frame.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-10-part-3.15d6d2849194.mp3",
        "duration": 9.048,
        "bytes": 72384,
        "sha256": "15d6d28491943bd673f1d4b61c01377eb4e9124b5e86f3dbb8d880b28f3d2ba3",
        "sourceHash": "8b971ef4cac6da564e73c08c1dba79bb27ea83414d1bab2283ce759fac392735"
      },
      {
        "text": "If the left arrow is down, subtract speed from the paddle position. If the right arrow is down, add speed instead.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-10-part-4.6850db8ba406.mp3",
        "duration": 7.896,
        "bytes": 63168,
        "sha256": "6850db8ba4067bef25dec0a48ce9e425978d72a2def3b9933c6ea7eb137840a4",
        "sourceHash": "694b3fc9e75348aa5378fe719d5d89e2739cb1cb25c38958850585ea59d12739"
      },
      {
        "text": "constrain keeps the paddle position between zero and the canvas width minus the paddle width. That way the whole paddle stays visible.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-10-part-5.41f8ff5d38f1.mp3",
        "duration": 8.904,
        "bytes": 71232,
        "sha256": "41f8ff5d38f16c507b21559200d80ec3ae03c215c68ffd200e66d5baa08838ef",
        "sourceHash": "1d81754c8aacf71068e53d61fdeaa7073aa4a9ef79fbd5a35ae17f5c7b32fb16"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try a speedy paddle or a wider one. Think about how each choice changes the feeling of control for a player.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-10-part-6.3e94427ffe59.mp3",
        "duration": 7.104,
        "bytes": 56832,
        "sha256": "3e94427ffe59d26a080b7d6134ca4000420976f0c5f19ba386fc1aa7ad75f0fc",
        "sourceHash": "26ed389514903dda0572d5e315755777fa48d9e65187b1d25e359a63bf16212a"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Imagine writing the same recipe every time you want a sandwich. A function lets you write the recipe once and give it a name.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-11-part-1.11a7eddf67d7.mp3",
        "duration": 8.424,
        "bytes": 67392,
        "sha256": "11a7eddf67d7727e12b6ab8b720de79be279667489e25ed4f72b8c9c6d812bb1",
        "sourceHash": "0651c2362eb77daba18e76c1189b3613019eed62686f57fa9e1e1c57f4a9b2a0"
      },
      {
        "text": "We create a function with the word function, a name, parentheses, and curly brackets. Inside the brackets are its instructions.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-11-part-2.0c43ae2f8c71.mp3",
        "duration": 8.4,
        "bytes": 67200,
        "sha256": "0c43ae2f8c71fda9c137866343403124427f6ca4db1d8784d90eb44f6b997588",
        "sourceHash": "248841a56978b5c13b2ae5c0352ee7e1d1b6fc768ab5bd7803d542ad41a357d5"
      },
      {
        "text": "Defining the function does not run it yet. Calling its name, with parentheses, tells the computer to follow the recipe.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-11-part-3.f9b258186303.mp3",
        "duration": 7.944,
        "bytes": 63552,
        "sha256": "f9b2581863039f4812f707357a2cb8d9e11d551c6b8a9955b431f2ed15c8ef71",
        "sourceHash": "b4e43759620af8cc613d76ea02ae2daa0f4d58ef7dbde24e3bcc83a509441082"
      },
      {
        "text": "Our draw function calls drawBall and drawPaddle. Each name tells us what that part of the program is responsible for.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-11-part-4.e7c3aec1b2dd.mp3",
        "duration": 7.8,
        "bytes": 62400,
        "sha256": "e7c3aec1b2dd6f91384e21f31ccc6b7982c718b042437a1dd1f7c09e1da9ef21",
        "sourceHash": "4080c62698adf6b1750930e72c8bfdf0064559e557ed9efcb1b3198b54ecdf50"
      },
      {
        "text": "Smaller functions make a bigger game easier to understand. If the ball looks wrong, you know which recipe to investigate.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-11-part-5.f06959aaf94f.mp3",
        "duration": 8.016,
        "bytes": 64128,
        "sha256": "f06959aaf94f37f5c66dcc9ea3bfde6bd2e900e3e4ec11da484a721359630fb6",
        "sourceHash": "02c919b9231855f731795868a529da26c21cdea0b4fea321d31649c6a79b8e52"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try changing just the ball recipe or the paddle recipe. Organizing your code is a creative skill, just like choosing a color.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-11-part-6.0c39dda0b08c.mp3",
        "duration": 8.784,
        "bytes": 70272,
        "sha256": "0c39dda0b08c5525f4c0f76a876ad4883f949f17cdab850115f7f27d68aa849a",
        "sourceHash": "0fe909c3b32cd171c6d874120bd7bcdd5f5ecf0a19dbee76ecfcac826069cd80"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A recipe becomes more useful when you can change its ingredients. Function parameters let the same instructions work with different values.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-12-part-1.de1ba9b20cc2.mp3",
        "duration": 9.024,
        "bytes": 72192,
        "sha256": "de1ba9b20cc2abadd7b5bc765563a01a6a763615a89ad7ea6278d3ba90a20aa8",
        "sourceHash": "4b31e7f6a2439a5d0b5bf6f75be3594bca3945086fa43d84f7c55d40ba740218"
      },
      {
        "text": "Our drawBall function accepts three parameters: x, y, and size. They are like empty spaces waiting for ingredients.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-12-part-2.f56ebcea25d7.mp3",
        "duration": 8.256,
        "bytes": 66048,
        "sha256": "f56ebcea25d713e228dcd343cdf211fd3c7118cd2a969b49489d8a5429f8f0ea",
        "sourceHash": "955bcd90471c53ab433c441148e79a76e2e4f408f4208ec57c5aeaabc94fa766"
      },
      {
        "text": "When we call drawBall with numbers, those numbers become the parameter values for that particular call. These supplied values are called arguments.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-12-part-3.58ae3448e9d8.mp3",
        "duration": 9.096,
        "bytes": 72768,
        "sha256": "58ae3448e9d8916bf7671822b1ca273a0caa2030d8156daf5d659b79ffd7904b",
        "sourceHash": "11367e36b15d4c22c648e0d6a6378207746220ef87390659ddfd1395606ca5e4"
      },
      {
        "text": "The first call might draw a small ball on the left. The next can draw a giant ball on the right using the same recipe.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-12-part-4.cfe6219ccf99.mp3",
        "duration": 7.656,
        "bytes": 61248,
        "sha256": "cfe6219ccf99d6d8f7b7fd26162b10749bd2fa37b205c33ef53437eed398a837",
        "sourceHash": "2dee3ddbc7b26231d535f234d5087ab532d4fe5ee2def7a46bac261a27273ba2"
      },
      {
        "text": "The names inside the function belong to that function. Each call gets its own inputs, so one call does not change another.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-12-part-5.8d5568589d15.mp3",
        "duration": 7.92,
        "bytes": 63360,
        "sha256": "8d5568589d15e8857cfe8b71acd4fd2a85bcb926a1012ae3b62ae4f974e90247",
        "sourceHash": "70847cc05e1415720db2e7f558fa2b0eaf0d357378a46bad34e8b55079a9dfd7"
      },
      {
        "text": "Add a new call and choose three new numbers. Instead of copying drawing steps, you are describing what you want your reusable tool to make.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-12-part-6.e0da3fb93361.mp3",
        "duration": 8.664,
        "bytes": 69312,
        "sha256": "e0da3fb93361ad52b0a1a670127ab1b4dd4b9048094a1a7751eefe9ceacb8130",
        "sourceHash": "9b62ed817a6b08696eec341ee8d94553481e88f54a62b8fb45c4cebabf2a77e2"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Drawing one circle is easy. Drawing a hundred by copying the same line is a lot of work. A loop lets the computer handle the repeating.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-13-part-1.543a8c1e816b.mp3",
        "duration": 9.216,
        "bytes": 73728,
        "sha256": "543a8c1e816b769710e772f88497da7cd5e6938c2ed39ec8a6952c2fc68d2436",
        "sourceHash": "414108a77fe951012d425fda1d8330ea42eb91c3ae82593bed07442489613431"
      },
      {
        "text": "A for loop has three controls. The first creates a counter, usually called i, and chooses where it starts.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-13-part-2.c8d88b9b7ad8.mp3",
        "duration": 7.104,
        "bytes": 56832,
        "sha256": "c8d88b9b7ad816a2b01aec5398cff0c3c6d6895c2f94e018054365fa8f970760",
        "sourceHash": "c6a993462d5be17fa626c5b0f723ea09b837f7faab9eb10f4507c74e4c51da00"
      },
      {
        "text": "The second is a condition. As long as the condition is true, the instructions inside the loop run again.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-13-part-3.f33d25a4569f.mp3",
        "duration": 7.032,
        "bytes": 56256,
        "sha256": "f33d25a4569f3b660542a755b5cbcd1f73ddae7bea423a55064ae5d6f7908a78",
        "sourceHash": "43edf0cb8c79ecf7cba91928cb456dd2dbf3518a5c07aa245520a3066359ac41"
      },
      {
        "text": "The third updates the counter. i plus plus adds one. Then the computer checks the condition and begins the next repeat.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-13-part-4.937778865810.mp3",
        "duration": 7.968,
        "bytes": 63744,
        "sha256": "937778865810e8348fe63a50c88d0de814b07bfd4ac2235c67085e52f5832aef",
        "sourceHash": "fb4695582d88b4fce879f1b09e43332a8782d7ff58f63660da081dfedc392607"
      },
      {
        "text": "We use the changing counter to choose each circle position. The same drawing instruction makes a neat row of different circles.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-13-part-5.0d8ace2eb6a2.mp3",
        "duration": 8.304,
        "bytes": 66432,
        "sha256": "0d8ace2eb6a2378c773602efd215811811daf73bb3b3bfd4d076379af9e3cea7",
        "sourceHash": "a043f3b10a344c34dbb86b312c123e19309e78a2d0ed08b4411a5394ae97afc7"
      },
      {
        "text": "Always make sure a loop can finish. Start with a small count, look at the result, and build up your pattern one repeat at a time.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-13-part-6.595b26a64295.mp3",
        "duration": 8.136,
        "bytes": 65088,
        "sha256": "595b26a642950730f3640202f078ef645937c6fddef9fcde543c1e1d1d3e98f7",
        "sourceHash": "f137911fa83abc80c4f162bf8bc495f743767b5386cf1f611b64dfc605b40368"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "You have stored one number in a variable. But what if you want to remember a different size for every ball? An array keeps a list.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-14-part-1.a2d5c874512a.mp3",
        "duration": 7.704,
        "bytes": 61632,
        "sha256": "a2d5c874512afb5923633a8668136116948c8a95cd72bd70da96023e39de8f76",
        "sourceHash": "fdf6df80a248d018c7801560e209e524d1c8232b38b167fdd6c4ccd1e59fd79d"
      },
      {
        "text": "Write the list inside square brackets and separate the items with commas. Our sizes array holds several different circle diameters.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-14-part-2.b142d7996ed3.mp3",
        "duration": 8.688,
        "bytes": 69504,
        "sha256": "b142d7996ed3983ff31ebadb7f8e1ce23ae2d0a2c81d7dd4fbc044935ab9d8dc",
        "sourceHash": "e377945f3bc800101e57a513cc444cbc6b4ba2091e605a63c1b213528e18774d"
      },
      {
        "text": "Every item has an address called an index. The first item is at index zero, the second at one, and so on.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-14-part-3.33cf6a9edbfa.mp3",
        "duration": 7.512,
        "bytes": 60096,
        "sha256": "33cf6a9edbfaf4b1a1025a560954ff5b1c1916577c99acb789a7c29163deb2d8",
        "sourceHash": "8571cadbfa29cb74109b3f772e03958d53f294a1080019f4c86ef5a7cc03a80b"
      },
      {
        "text": "sizes with i in square brackets looks up the item at index i. A loop can visit each item and use its value.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-14-part-4.5e37bf5d9067.mp3",
        "duration": 8.04,
        "bytes": 64320,
        "sha256": "5e37bf5d906709627cc04f54ab23d41f081ef9cdfdda0250edcda84e2730acb6",
        "sourceHash": "af5b312edcd7b82da6b9f492279d241d26c0178a9a39014563c03d5cafbc0d61"
      },
      {
        "text": "The length property tells us how many items the array contains. Use it as the loop limit, so adding an item also adds a drawing.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-14-part-5.1d580dae1587.mp3",
        "duration": 8.736,
        "bytes": 69888,
        "sha256": "1d580dae1587ea114daca5ef5ccf27d2eccaae64d4ec7de8f4a901f849aedab1",
        "sourceHash": "cab5585878353dbd8c51c157282cdcd7728dface60750a32d5ed57e00b0e401e"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try making a pattern with small and big values. Your array is a tiny collection of creative choices that your loop brings to life.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-14-part-6.bca02e96de27.mp3",
        "duration": 8.64,
        "bytes": 69120,
        "sha256": "bca02e96de27083d8dbc4f60081a5498b22d121f3518d5ae09cd2ef0e983dd51",
        "sourceHash": "4f09130acbc78356a46af3711bd88bbce0d5e9cf49aa7d1603fcb0d6d460d584"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A brick breaker needs a whole wall of bricks. One loop can make a row, but two loops working together can make the entire wall.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-15-part-1.e10d45739e15.mp3",
        "duration": 8.064,
        "bytes": 64512,
        "sha256": "e10d45739e15ea64b87a706613d631edafa38cfcf16de14d26e81131ab6d48f4",
        "sourceHash": "f53d1043cc766e798abc53e10fe5269d0941139dbd4ce03d0d16ff0f356dd21c"
      },
      {
        "text": "The outer loop counts the rows. Each time it runs, it starts the inner loop from the beginning.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-15-part-2.2783d12a311d.mp3",
        "duration": 5.928,
        "bytes": 47424,
        "sha256": "2783d12a311dc7ad38d32652a76b2e53d5224607eaf23c6877dd2fcd0c7a400f",
        "sourceHash": "89166414cbff506baade03e5c46393a1d22899f2f25abd246ce2845bc816339b"
      },
      {
        "text": "The inner loop counts the columns. It draws one brick for each column, then finishes so the outer loop can move to the next row.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-15-part-3.163af3508d94.mp3",
        "duration": 8.064,
        "bytes": 64512,
        "sha256": "163af3508d9419ff3512adbd160772088827c3a42f41dcad8c6933ead0d21a22",
        "sourceHash": "682e3eae11d47cd8dd0e5be62e42906f7dac22fb22f59a2a320597dc8aaaa69f"
      },
      {
        "text": "Use the column number to calculate x, and the row number to calculate y. Multiplication creates the spacing between neighboring bricks.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-15-part-4.b627620e8e79.mp3",
        "duration": 9.12,
        "bytes": 72960,
        "sha256": "b627620e8e799d0ecea154bd5fef8b39ac55473ed85aebecc426d01162f723f2",
        "sourceHash": "759cb6fd59724f843d4024f966d296298527064bb01b9709c9bc27fd50bf3039"
      },
      {
        "text": "Three rows of six columns gives eighteen bricks. Changing either number changes the whole wall, without copying any drawing instructions.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-15-part-5.59f4e985c2cd.mp3",
        "duration": 8.808,
        "bytes": 70464,
        "sha256": "59f4e985c2cd4955a78e11aa8f1224a0d9890d0d9ebb542485f1796d1d8a7f68",
        "sourceHash": "dbd59a0d90f38e8c0e6fa97a2edcb201f85e03c75d6bed3d05a1935f759e5ff7"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try a tall wall or a wide wall. Nested loops are useful for maps, board games, pixel art, and all kinds of repeating worlds.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-15-part-6.639428837f72.mp3",
        "duration": 8.856,
        "bytes": 70848,
        "sha256": "639428837f727d83f23ec4cc74b1f94bef03ef9e73fcebfcd4ea8f0334dbfe17",
        "sourceHash": "31417743a10e01c480b7142e812e2412d275d7b5a6a846ca0c49723508e2f219"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Our ball needs a position, a size, a color, and eventually a velocity. An object keeps those related details together as one thing.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-16-part-1.62fa766704d5.mp3",
        "duration": 9.384,
        "bytes": 75072,
        "sha256": "62fa766704d574c86cbb2c8d928edf957d8c7d0ee66b3f3cb624fe62d5c2e786",
        "sourceHash": "59b989146b96862ff33425434597f4518c47198de362b676083f21afe7de8b17"
      },
      {
        "text": "Create an object with curly brackets. Inside, give each property a name, a colon, and a value, separating the properties with commas.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-16-part-2.5c857698f2b2.mp3",
        "duration": 8.976,
        "bytes": 71808,
        "sha256": "5c857698f2b28d15fa3eafe61f02e9254776ad02bc1d41c277c06c6c15d18108",
        "sourceHash": "b61adf4726f648b00c810a0b99a8916349bbce82b8b54238c2c27c847898b74e"
      },
      {
        "text": "The property x belongs to the ball object. We write ball dot x to read it. That dot means look inside this object.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-16-part-3.ee7cb6f9f96d.mp3",
        "duration": 7.848,
        "bytes": 62784,
        "sha256": "ee7cb6f9f96d93a6ef77b711177926a9c4cfe6c5837b88d62a6ba46513d154aa",
        "sourceHash": "efd951c4b84d2b030592728490800d63bbdbacb3648f4268ebb1b401020eaca0"
      },
      {
        "text": "You can change a property just like a variable. Updating ball dot x moves the ball without changing its size or color.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-16-part-4.79371c1983e4.mp3",
        "duration": 8.28,
        "bytes": 66240,
        "sha256": "79371c1983e474d5f4becd6adf859f90b3b9acb37e5694fc337200c492b6e9af",
        "sourceHash": "db698d8079a3116912db2289d0570e543658a0dc02207219ffddc567c8f8477f"
      },
      {
        "text": "An array can hold objects too. Soon, each brick in our wall will have its own position and a property that remembers whether it is alive.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-16-part-5.fd77cb78c2e6.mp3",
        "duration": 9.048,
        "bytes": 72384,
        "sha256": "fd77cb78c2e629daf96201750b8bd9f55a3bbdd06dbb611fc42488aee76433ac",
        "sourceHash": "be61a08c351e61edba0593965e9a8ccfd31be9dac24e3cbdc260d7ac8fa1d2f4"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try changing a few ball properties. Notice how the drawing code stays clear: it describes the ball using the information the ball already owns.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-16-part-6.58022389e14a.mp3",
        "duration": 9.12,
        "bytes": 72960,
        "sha256": "58022389e14af25eef227a5fa122f6936a6445e5b397176bb21425f17870d0c1",
        "sourceHash": "82fc4bf7a2be9f0b29b6b21122ed3f00f90e579c57e3f2fb8448d2cdd2d4b4c8"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "An object describes one thing. A class is a blueprint that helps us create many similar things, each with its own information.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-17-part-1.0571ec85085d.mp3",
        "duration": 8.52,
        "bytes": 68160,
        "sha256": "0571ec85085d97b79e16dcb63df3374e5b0f9b40fa3e9152124b0f8b64f08f11",
        "sourceHash": "1ae4608a2353210bc068440b578f008b8a7f1662103090dd615b378203634e78"
      },
      {
        "text": "The constructor runs when we use new Ball. It receives the starting values and saves them on the new object.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-17-part-2.188b8edded3f.mp3",
        "duration": 7.056,
        "bytes": 56448,
        "sha256": "188b8edded3f70fb8d1c6e9b8d757ebe21c920b7397e00897f771c08e19ef97e",
        "sourceHash": "6552154fd3f2d34933c3e9ffc9921b8c47de912dc0bb98aed2751b6f16766e03"
      },
      {
        "text": "The word this means this particular object. this dot size is the size of the ball we are working with, not every ball.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-17-part-3.37039a8b8b6e.mp3",
        "duration": 7.944,
        "bytes": 63552,
        "sha256": "37039a8b8b6e5198300acf085f15386091401fa396340d1a2db7b8d49d37f95f",
        "sourceHash": "d64da723affb0244a9de86c460c1926c074deca244b1051ad9e361905e60e0ec"
      },
      {
        "text": "A method is a function inside a class. Our display method knows how to draw a ball using its own position and size.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-17-part-4.bfe9f64e52b4.mp3",
        "duration": 7.92,
        "bytes": 63360,
        "sha256": "bfe9f64e52b4cb0ee32a8df6b92ae3ab5e9f899550cb8e9f9225dddf1a26ea11",
        "sourceHash": "7aadf021783a16fbde1028ed4f0bb3d0109383816eeb1e1be46dc3d24eca313b"
      },
      {
        "text": "We create several balls and store them in an array. Then a loop asks each ball to display itself. Each object follows the same blueprint.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-17-part-5.9ae79dda2efc.mp3",
        "duration": 9.096,
        "bytes": 72768,
        "sha256": "9ae79dda2efc91859367d46eb6cf49f652ad1d883176d2fe58c7f9324da7a392",
        "sourceHash": "af6b0b764d048c50890b2b495e9dad84a8dd988432ee6fee183493d04378ca26"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try changing one ball size, then adding another ball. Blueprints make it easier to create a whole cast of characters without copying all their instructions.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-17-part-6.3d529a0f6926.mp3",
        "duration": 10.296,
        "bytes": 82368,
        "sha256": "3d529a0f6926c7f5370186f9016b5e9bd0c94e919e023de696576279f6ae1be0",
        "sourceHash": "fbd0c0b461d19cdbb38be9b8618855ec88d7e925a2dd48d117c46c3d2d3f2cbb"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Games become more interesting when something unexpected can happen. random lets your computer choose a number from a range you decide.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-18-part-1.f5edec5a514c.mp3",
        "duration": 8.4,
        "bytes": 67200,
        "sha256": "f5edec5a514cbd3c21f5480715cb95172d85375f3ac6ff918ef7d9f386ee85f5",
        "sourceHash": "7878bb21f26b08fa84d5606ae56ff0f8681559152a16279b79c265cf3c5c70ac"
      },
      {
        "text": "random with two numbers chooses a value starting at the first number and stopping just short of the second. The answer can include decimals.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-18-part-2.bfa3fbc6a690.mp3",
        "duration": 8.904,
        "bytes": 71232,
        "sha256": "bfa3fbc6a69026458169d310f633f3abf8cf36b745702db33657ad27c39991bf",
        "sourceHash": "db291c7c39df3914a808170051e7799d8067ab9a2362616d896909c983241c45"
      },
      {
        "text": "Pick the position inside the canvas edges, and a ball will always appear on screen. Boundaries make randomness useful instead of confusing.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-18-part-3.ce985e20b22e.mp3",
        "duration": 9.48,
        "bytes": 75840,
        "sha256": "ce985e20b22e1cb1eb6bfca93b2516085550580a420ffab3c0c68f1d81948ef5",
        "sourceHash": "85bfb4fd94f50f8812179ca0750a9d5b34cba8fd7fa8a47ecb4d4e883ae68463"
      },
      {
        "text": "We make our random choices in a function called shuffle. setup calls it once, and mousePressed calls it again whenever you click.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-18-part-4.da05ee17c4b0.mp3",
        "duration": 8.088,
        "bytes": 64704,
        "sha256": "da05ee17c4b01661371a43d7eac5ed126e7d96892064c99d6aca92ee99a316ed",
        "sourceHash": "f1c3dd5e5aafefbe715cd746d15bcfbe42e92a79d53f8d75b300e0f30662b998"
      },
      {
        "text": "If you put random directly inside draw, you choose a new value every frame. That can create flicker instead of a stable surprise.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-18-part-5.1639ef957692.mp3",
        "duration": 8.688,
        "bytes": 69504,
        "sha256": "1639ef9576920f30e9e36f259f5825cb8dc8ab29d927f8882bbe4f380a39903e",
        "sourceHash": "f6e5e0eda277326c7fa03ce3d8e76128425323bacf8b1179f6f6c18c3e909c51"
      },
      {
        "text": "Click to generate several arrangements. Then change the limits. You are not choosing every result, but you are designing the space of possible results.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-18-part-6.7139e1417774.mp3",
        "duration": 9.576,
        "bytes": 76608,
        "sha256": "7139e1417774dfabc03eb0456bce099aa8ff63fdffaf26c55879374c511c7d11",
        "sourceHash": "eafce931e6f07cace230b8faea9086486bc3cd9fcb29b4d16f543cf2a3a7fc47"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A brick cannot break until the game knows it has been touched. Collision detection is how we turn touching into a question our code can answer.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-19-part-1.52e12acda902.mp3",
        "duration": 9.192,
        "bytes": 73536,
        "sha256": "52e12acda902a223d7cd875dba840f36fa8ebc485ccac71e0f346961f0f9c271",
        "sourceHash": "2fa92213b40df743e728bd17a7f037fefb04f20ad7e7c9110dec6108a9bbcb0c"
      },
      {
        "text": "The circle has a center and a radius. The rectangle has edges. First, find the point on the rectangle closest to the circle center.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-19-part-2.35d820e12b3c.mp3",
        "duration": 8.976,
        "bytes": 71808,
        "sha256": "35d820e12b3c078e3dab661791c533f797c659188b7ddcd61e8a4f7b14518241",
        "sourceHash": "b5db789ede1e3de61b9b028637aa6c73dbd9043ff2055b6d6ee2523b5b506093"
      },
      {
        "text": "constrain clamps the circle x coordinate between the left and right edges. Do the same with y between the top and bottom edges.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-19-part-3.4f1a1bba38d8.mp3",
        "duration": 8.592,
        "bytes": 68736,
        "sha256": "4f1a1bba38d851eb9710d2b43a6f4c061822d0d62d1577fa45de6bb00465e6ec",
        "sourceHash": "09336331f03974e4ee4ff09b253d6b3323e07c4bf605714f09a7ca576e9a0d98"
      },
      {
        "text": "Now dist measures the distance from the ball center to that nearest point. If it is smaller than the radius, the variable touching becomes true.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-19-part-4.2d53ae25c12e.mp3",
        "duration": 9.048,
        "bytes": 72384,
        "sha256": "2d53ae25c12e46c8d31790db3b8203edb3a3fb94f1bf3b1233b3e7b50147443d",
        "sourceHash": "b831597c3a993922b67c5ecb6cb1b41e3f60bf07ef3c0a21799e94d9d3a3c70b"
      },
      {
        "text": "A variable can hold true or false, just like it holds a number. Our if statement reads touching and picks the brick color.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-19-part-5.c4253b0a4de2.mp3",
        "duration": 7.752,
        "bytes": 62016,
        "sha256": "c4253b0a4de2922567589bf533573da2cd1e395ab7546553cdf41d88b2ec9b8e",
        "sourceHash": "5743e0a822d7171b3942ba2537687bcead3e471668b84b39e2599fb39bcb4db5"
      },
      {
        "text": "Move the ball around the corners as well as the sides. Testing edges and corners helps us discover whether a collision rule works everywhere.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-19-part-6.95e556a5e692.mp3",
        "duration": 9.144,
        "bytes": 73152,
        "sha256": "95e556a5e692e2c12ffdc52d8d7dc842be23b4ae6f845f4973f9d43878135102",
        "sourceHash": "622a0ff34a763e33370b8fef764d69caa86dbb44d193b1b7358574a1a2683033"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "It is time to combine our ideas. A moving ball, a paddle that follows your mouse, and a collision rule. Together they become the start of your game.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-20-part-1.eba7e7d6f278.mp3",
        "duration": 9.384,
        "bytes": 75072,
        "sha256": "eba7e7d6f27891342522bfbe1dc1894d1736f3b15eac2e400693dac80812d8d3",
        "sourceHash": "4959628c12ee9132b0e34946ad3d87cf7495eef510504e6d688c8f62ab7f743c"
      },
      {
        "text": "Look at draw. It reads like a to do list: move the paddle, move the ball, check the paddle, check for a miss. Each job is its own function.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-20-part-2.9d7c95f55ce0.mp3",
        "duration": 8.688,
        "bytes": 69504,
        "sha256": "9d7c95f55ce03c2a3ea8ffb8ef0e07b78c20f50d0e13b88e53b6cca810585bd2",
        "sourceHash": "f580caf374988355236be33c7db7ba2f531c5cbe8754d11bcb6a2a68b2d5a41c"
      },
      {
        "text": "The ball has two speeds. speedX moves it across, and speedY moves it up or down. Adding both every frame makes it travel diagonally.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-20-part-3.ba362a40ecf9.mp3",
        "duration": 9.576,
        "bytes": 76608,
        "sha256": "ba362a40ecf9d2cbf252966a8d6e6e6be9785a3720e66b8e69fe1f3c317ff0ba",
        "sourceHash": "3a50befb10b49ff7bfd549fac48c3254e9fde84b9ec6b9321269329eb1be71b3"
      },
      {
        "text": "In bounceOffPaddle, three true or false questions must all be true. Is the ball moving down? Is it low enough? Is it over the paddle?",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-20-part-4.f607c83cdc28.mp3",
        "duration": 8.928,
        "bytes": 71424,
        "sha256": "f607c83cdc28fefd86e4db4d13299609b5400fa55eb68e0834981662c917b7ca",
        "sourceHash": "ecabdb520556deb1b8203674389958bba5d9115fd6050a8471e30411e5bff3ce"
      },
      {
        "text": "Right now the ball does not bounce. Your job is to write the missing line: make speedY negative, so the ball flies back up.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-20-part-5.601d864d5909.mp3",
        "duration": 8.28,
        "bytes": 66240,
        "sha256": "601d864d5909840b3f3d158534979981a9e986469cf2763a214422f34227e8bf",
        "sourceHash": "afe916de67db44e0ca8be0e06a376b6f19af1ba9184219fbb7eccc2fb6ecb463"
      },
      {
        "text": "Do not worry if you miss. The ball resets so you can try again. Change the paddle width and discover how small choices change the challenge.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-20-part-6.ec124b954bb6.mp3",
        "duration": 9.144,
        "bytes": 73152,
        "sha256": "ec124b954bb650d6c20479ba02c01b584551b174cec9e1f04dd0b1689336d78e",
        "sourceHash": "9a889d6054caa1c78f8151fbc1e83bc124bb089ec87ec7feb9540d3813e27f73"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Your ball has a purpose now: clear a wall of bricks. Every brick is made from a Brick class, the blueprint idea from chapter seventeen.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-21-part-1.8713d8d763fa.mp3",
        "duration": 8.616,
        "bytes": 68928,
        "sha256": "8713d8d763fa526d84f15a125c0bfb986d6582af6ffbf2e1dd9b79d5be4ba361",
        "sourceHash": "a5edd2a6e0341dc2d540e0e2442a689081565fd7328a83956a346bdc23d8a794"
      },
      {
        "text": "Each brick remembers its position, its size, and an alive property that starts as true. makeBricks uses nested loops to build the whole wall.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-21-part-2.dd99e8a33601.mp3",
        "duration": 9.408,
        "bytes": 75264,
        "sha256": "dd99e8a33601859f74173e2f85aaf92ac67c9eb4588a0491094873f0ed38ab34",
        "sourceHash": "1f3971afd77637a8244ba10a7189f17e2e38e9e3c45a96a4ee54d45db7b3060a"
      },
      {
        "text": "In breakBricks, we visit every brick and use our nearest point test. If the ball overlaps a living brick, that brick has been hit.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-21-part-3.c557c44bc3cb.mp3",
        "duration": 8.04,
        "bytes": 64320,
        "sha256": "c557c44bc3cb2710932c906b4a5e27a69aebc4fce3e5924c041ac8cbf2a43a43",
        "sourceHash": "7d8fe82ae46f8942dc18d6138dd23b3cd7fa240c3857826d9ecbf91af5bb548f"
      },
      {
        "text": "Your job is to finish the hit. Set the brick alive property to false. Then drawBricks skips it, and it disappears from the world.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-21-part-4.f4a543d251e1.mp3",
        "duration": 8.448,
        "bytes": 67584,
        "sha256": "f4a543d251e15f92aab4b290a743f79cd786952dcd7a4e03fc2c1ccb56efd1ed",
        "sourceHash": "e700cac8067f01545c781dd1d17f2611f48a463b80decf7c41f3377e5997d51a"
      },
      {
        "text": "The word break stops the loop after one hit. That way the ball cannot bounce twice in one frame when it touches two bricks.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-21-part-5.767e6193f27d.mp3",
        "duration": 7.68,
        "bytes": 61440,
        "sha256": "767e6193f27dca2ccf2dd1708d3139511fc9327910602386a4fb65d8507fd280",
        "sourceHash": "99b2ed7d7bdc46c421c0b51bed4fcc6ebd3872860c1f04a793f10463a07ebd83"
      },
      {
        "text": "checkWallCleared counts the bricks that are still alive. When the count reaches zero, a fresh wall appears. Try different rows and columns!",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-21-part-6.6dfa879e255b.mp3",
        "duration": 8.88,
        "bytes": 71040,
        "sha256": "6dfa879e255be53d810e1d2f6630071c2224994658b40239290d3af93a7345aa",
        "sourceHash": "ae2deff8771ba14b14fbc00e7badbd8416d6013326a6a3ac16a06ff8de7afcbd"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Breaking bricks is satisfying, but how do you know how well you are doing? A score turns your progress into a number you can see.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-22-part-1.a9632c974d24.mp3",
        "duration": 8.016,
        "bytes": 64128,
        "sha256": "a9632c974d244d800a027bf622c93a86e5ddd847e0782fc7dc4d11cfe27bfdbb",
        "sourceHash": "b15d1e4ccba8741717ceee08947c6967ae46f91b06879cba233e55146a33033d"
      },
      {
        "text": "The score variable starts at zero. Your first job: when a brick breaks, add pointsPerBrick to score, right where the brick is hit.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-22-part-2.b1a240fe1c10.mp3",
        "duration": 8.136,
        "bytes": 65088,
        "sha256": "b1a240fe1c10f8674bd7af4eba1c708e6dad07382ef5343df5c0d1a4cc391d36",
        "sourceHash": "91f003bb3dbe946c1bf08d49b2c69a79ed4758358d86a95a60fedd79f7eb2bbe"
      },
      {
        "text": "The lives variable remembers how many chances remain. Your second job: take away one life when the ball falls below the canvas.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-22-part-3.b6c1efa366fc.mp3",
        "duration": 8.616,
        "bytes": 68928,
        "sha256": "b6c1efa366fc2704a661e5544745c11fb6e790757d94a48ce55938234c99331a",
        "sourceHash": "be78d8526fbcd8a268bb39a1a0aa08749d5d3a6b54c27a935dacc90064178adb"
      },
      {
        "text": "drawScore uses text to show the numbers. The plus sign joins words and numbers together into one message for the player.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-22-part-4.cc49ed25e0db.mp3",
        "duration": 8.064,
        "bytes": 64512,
        "sha256": "cc49ed25e0dbe55354dcb7e6db63feb683055257a71a0e5e8593375294d5a89b",
        "sourceHash": "8b4fe5f6d16ffb21849ed030c847d16a8ce95e314d3a56ed36ea2322f6e57451"
      },
      {
        "text": "startingLives remembers how many lives a new game begins with. When lives reach zero, restartGame uses it to start fresh.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-22-part-5.5ddd9bb6745a.mp3",
        "duration": 8.664,
        "bytes": 69312,
        "sha256": "5ddd9bb6745ac57c977c6b6943d662a9574f6f54d15216e14db6a79a18c81ad9",
        "sourceHash": "180c97f89ae570dd59da5a2b8b2a0744691facce3c400f72bd994898e85e1ea2"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try changing the reward for a brick or giving yourself more lives. Notice how visible feedback helps you understand what your code is doing.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-22-part-6.a26af3b441e0.mp3",
        "duration": 9.048,
        "bytes": 72384,
        "sha256": "a26af3b441e0ed6f06ee16800aa4eb781cb52172e14f8969061f3912f4fe9e73",
        "sourceHash": "3ba9d67135894c0f1d9909b13a2067ad02c54e2b60726a570f71e1d7aa815f7d"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "A game needs a beginning, a middle, and an ending. A state variable remembers which part of the experience is happening right now.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-23-part-1.ff7d8a64812c.mp3",
        "duration": 8.616,
        "bytes": 68928,
        "sha256": "ff7d8a64812cd54efdaac9223f291c8fe4294aa85d3377addfa8e3d9490cc26c",
        "sourceHash": "53b2847ec51e8c2736804a722dadba2ffab886f3821c4629aeb4a282817a178c"
      },
      {
        "text": "Our states are ready, playing, over, and won. They are strings, words inside quotes, that our if statements can compare.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-23-part-2.51d48c707e6d.mp3",
        "duration": 7.584,
        "bytes": 60672,
        "sha256": "51d48c707e6df2bb8ff070cbb3a68c8fe45f59b13249dff50f58bf534680b5b0",
        "sourceHash": "057bd769ef6fe41b7b756cc749ef369aa32bd29b30baae21750fbecb920c56c1"
      },
      {
        "text": "Look at draw. The ball only moves when state is playing. In the ready state, the game waits and shows a message instead.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-23-part-3.6021a606430d.mp3",
        "duration": 7.656,
        "bytes": 61248,
        "sha256": "6021a606430d03277067a3e6df45fbdeed63aba5402d090dcf536b09ba7c4299",
        "sourceHash": "d58f281f7c74d37ed8df67a11fab01027f3aab9cfb482c5894c643e56f9f3a30"
      },
      {
        "text": "mousePressed runs once every time you click. Your first job: when state is ready, a click should change state to playing.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-23-part-4.a685c1f1b744.mp3",
        "duration": 7.896,
        "bytes": 63168,
        "sha256": "a685c1f1b744aef1a7a1d71d682e6f2dbd1e0f8117e76afd3688ba3f3cfa71d1",
        "sourceHash": "249ce4b0f72e8a298c163382c1f20cf7e245fe6fdbab77a2bc46e6f1e9c4fa3f"
      },
      {
        "text": "Your second job: when lives run out, set state to over. drawMessage notices and shows the ending screen. Another click starts again.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-23-part-5.64d920b6e766.mp3",
        "duration": 9.24,
        "bytes": 73920,
        "sha256": "64d920b6e766797ef8419aff3754266119105cbe3abbef2df9fcf821433180c8",
        "sourceHash": "88f23fc1260e419de0f51b7790f763c98a157bab1f0cf240fe8ad3876ec7930a"
      },
      {
        "text": "Follow each change from one state to the next. States are like rooms in a house, and your code decides which door leads where.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-23-part-6.453d99647a0c.mp3",
        "duration": 8.136,
        "bytes": 65088,
        "sha256": "453d99647a0c49e6ec19a8e85f2dae612a3aed72eb731601bfa85542a3e9e702",
        "sourceHash": "5180f6c3836750cf1adb53e628d4fe3958aeb0e7d9cbe1696c7313014dbd1bc4"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "The rules work, but a great game also feels good. Let us add a burst of tiny sparks whenever the ball breaks a brick.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-24-part-1.aba43b3c039e.mp3",
        "duration": 7.68,
        "bytes": 61440,
        "sha256": "aba43b3c039e2feb88515b2cc04e62be97d13cb4a3bfc1fece4fdf02b52fc92b",
        "sourceHash": "cae706aef70456d392935cb44395ffe3cebadd477aba153b14a1dcfc314c2501"
      },
      {
        "text": "Each spark is made from the Particle class. It has a position, a random speed, and a life counter that starts at thirty.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-24-part-2.932616cc9ba2.mp3",
        "duration": 8.088,
        "bytes": 64704,
        "sha256": "932616cc9ba2b890b80075394a6f88917d48b26af754b472f7f9fadcb2748f63",
        "sourceHash": "ba02c6ecf6bf98bf38de10f7426db88c700509fe12864b937d9a558302102f6a"
      },
      {
        "text": "burst runs a loop when a brick breaks. Your job is to fill that loop: push a new Particle into the particles array each time around.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-24-part-3.a76997e15885.mp3",
        "duration": 8.688,
        "bytes": 69504,
        "sha256": "a76997e15885e9947dfe18185bc77ebc45b24cc76ddbab6486142d8638d298ac",
        "sourceHash": "f60306943e63a3f366688d433164fa1e1140809f13da4654e2423323284279aa"
      },
      {
        "text": "Every frame, each particle moves and loses one life. The fourth fill number is see through-ness, so the sparks fade as life runs out.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-24-part-4.19b4a49ed87b.mp3",
        "duration": 8.76,
        "bytes": 70080,
        "sha256": "19b4a49ed87b9dc86cb5af7ab1d69c0c3bb4eb62dcd559e68a002942b2adcc23",
        "sourceHash": "e65bba4458433c409d5603ebb7665d0ed4466e958af89bc4bdf37db4340eefd3"
      },
      {
        "text": "drawParticles keeps only the sparks that still have life, in a new array called stillAlive. The old sparks are left behind and disappear.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-24-part-5.932825ce58ac.mp3",
        "duration": 9.36,
        "bytes": 74880,
        "sha256": "932825ce58ac171b49040eb32bbc3e6dfd452aeaee511718eae164fdac14b5cf",
        "sourceHash": "7275742979830a0567b339af13e7645342a200769db8d1d2c69bfce56ecd4aa0"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try a bigger burst and watch the effect. Good game feel uses a little feedback to make a small moment feel clear and rewarding.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-24-part-6.eab98263d430.mp3",
        "duration": 8.304,
        "bytes": 66432,
        "sha256": "eab98263d43079f56670cb75cfe8fd693cedff38a873109a0f7f26066c9d90b8",
        "sourceHash": "1cb7d376c07105c64fc4e7e1cb84580a4df890e38f9eb5cdb6150b2c683889a5"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "You can finish a wall. What happens next? Levels give the player a new goal and let the game grow a little more challenging.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-25-part-1.e3db665b1efb.mp3",
        "duration": 7.536,
        "bytes": 60288,
        "sha256": "e3db665b1efb377b362dac2850f3c165bf451ff8a80ed5fe46c52f2a6eae227e",
        "sourceHash": "20ec2934394370e651f67c951f67931563a2e726d0b6e0f7e59a7c287665c030"
      },
      {
        "text": "checkWallCleared counts the bricks that are still alive. The counter starts at zero and adds one for every living brick it finds.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-25-part-2.b9cf13dcdac7.mp3",
        "duration": 8.088,
        "bytes": 64704,
        "sha256": "b9cf13dcdac7deb47651be85bf9e5be8d6aa8fe76d4ee661e894bf0f75355aae",
        "sourceHash": "50f7074c11c47d29f7db6855b29d1cbe784598515745cff9dd93a559727992df"
      },
      {
        "text": "When the count is zero, the wall is clear. Your job is to add one to level. Then the game builds a new wall and resets the ball.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-25-part-3.b87957addf5e.mp3",
        "duration": 8.52,
        "bytes": 68160,
        "sha256": "b87957addf5ecf218e54efff5c5ec028c022935f60016447dbd3ff2e0c052057",
        "sourceHash": "9cb9e0d5bfb08d6ed49517fa42a695f2ca67bccf8ea59c25d662b222029938a9"
      },
      {
        "text": "The new speed uses the level number and levelBoost. A small boost makes each level a gentle step up instead of a sudden jump.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-25-part-4.eb2d933ee04c.mp3",
        "duration": 8.352,
        "bytes": 66816,
        "sha256": "eb2d933ee04c16754c220de1053cc6a9af2450f43b094a83395a0834ba0feb9a",
        "sourceHash": "541c186b8c0a6a001df43ad6959350005b54467bc34e89c7c84f394c32f80c6c"
      },
      {
        "text": "The game returns to ready before the next launch. That short pause gives the player time to notice the new level and get ready.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-25-part-5.af9a5e7e6267.mp3",
        "duration": 7.992,
        "bytes": 63936,
        "sha256": "af9a5e7e62677e7db935d1ffd7d001d096ac357b824bcd18b0b1862819e02160",
        "sourceHash": "3901c975ed2318d66cb970af8c4229c99512355b87e84dd7c7775ff00f1e21ba"
      },
      {
        "text": "Try a very small boost, then a larger one. Test the feeling, not just the numbers. Difficulty is something you design for a person.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-25-part-6.f9bbab73bf91.mp3",
        "duration": 8.976,
        "bytes": 71808,
        "sha256": "f9bbab73bf91aa15f6345e52a8541c668879f5c44a03c72509bbb76bb7de5d1a",
        "sourceHash": "96b67df7adebac3288376eb776fccee5a8d7f3b143d922b4aeece441544c4de2"
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Look at what you have built. Your first circle has become a complete brick breaker, with a paddle, collisions, scores, lives, levels, and sparks.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-26-part-1.0d441e64d66e.mp3",
        "duration": 9.384,
        "bytes": 75072,
        "sha256": "0d441e64d66eea85fc6a90131394e678c754fdf3560a39f64d218da299765a5a",
        "sourceHash": "f28a6ef62c392f02c38ae01dc87c1146378ffc9f27f438e589d18043ec3e39cb"
      },
      {
        "text": "Variables remember the world. Arrays collect the bricks. Classes are blueprints for bricks and sparks. Functions give every job a name.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-26-part-2.a6b5caf8b9d2.mp3",
        "duration": 9.192,
        "bytes": 73536,
        "sha256": "a6b5caf8b9d230c186728867d2a097ae03dc7ff46313468f30d49c8e76d41229",
        "sourceHash": "963f5180df3dab1258dc3d873c628a6eb41a93b5aa3c1b4f705db8da697ce9e8"
      },
      {
        "text": "The draw loop brings those pieces to life. Input lets you play, collision rules create consequences, and states give the game a beginning and an ending.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-26-part-3.e1ef3b7233bb.mp3",
        "duration": 9.984,
        "bytes": 79872,
        "sha256": "e1ef3b7233bbac96b754a48b8ac8e76b03fd35582962857c6b60d82b50ba18d6",
        "sourceHash": "39a96d123dee229fe0da3f8bc08ba41651a080f8931e156ef83a6103241d67a6"
      },
      {
        "text": "Now make it yours. Choose your colors, change the wall, and decide what kind of challenge you want another player to experience.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-26-part-4.5def52a28539.mp3",
        "duration": 7.944,
        "bytes": 63552,
        "sha256": "5def52a28539b7b4b10abbfbc5daa4767da27c6375af5bd4cd7543f0164c100d",
        "sourceHash": "90e22d40e8d8bef73a1bd26c6d5f0adf91a74d7f1625ccd166dd42778cd07895"
      },
      {
        "text": "Ask someone to play. Watch where they smile and where they seem confused. Their experience can teach you what to improve next.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-26-part-5.07a13f2fd47b.mp3",
        "duration": 7.728,
        "bytes": 61824,
        "sha256": "07a13f2fd47ba9b9e96246c59077e83c8a44bca262dbbab7c0ff39b51cbfefb7",
        "sourceHash": "b4a3781682e311990f4d6baede19a403cacea3e7adc60a46080837aaf782f25c"
      },
      {
        "text": "You do not have to know everything to make something wonderful. Keep asking what happens if, change one idea, and build your next project one brick at a time.",
        "src": "audio/narration/chapter-26-part-6.54bb8a0e32cd.mp3",
        "duration": 9.84,
        "bytes": 78720,
        "sha256": "54bb8a0e32cda975d972ba49a8ac7d15a2da0f5f9987103d5fa546222e28df8d",
        "sourceHash": "3b4ab5c1f97b9841923cf8ebdd1ef9a5975264036a37136e7e2990907a2749ff"
      }
    ]
  ]
};
